Z||000000||DaveL||09-10-2001||03:20 PM||||I'm about to change the oil pan gasket on a used 2.5 I just bought, and I'm wondering what would be the best thing to flush the inside of the engine with while it's apart?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||09-10-2001||09:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaveL:<BR><B>I'm about to change the oil pan gasket on a used 2.5 I just bought, and I'm wondering what would be the best thing to flush the inside of the engine with while it's apart?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Before pulling the pan, add a quart of motor flush and follow directions...<BR>If after pulling the pan, there is sludge, scrape it out, and clean with kerosene...<BR>I doubt you will find any sludge, unless the previous owner used non-detergent oil...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Leadfoot||09-10-2001||11:24 PM||||I agree with Fierowrecker, but I went a step further and used some aircraft paint remover for the stubborn areas.  It works like a charm.||||reg||1
